; expect 24
; A WRITTEN instance wins at the call, and only its absence reaches
; the builtin - the resolution order phase 0 of the traits removal
; promised, pinned here with a trait of this file's own so that the
; pin outlives `stdlib/Show.ax`'s deprecation (an `impl` of THAT trait
; draws AX3048, and the corpus the compiler must find nothing to
; report in cannot carry one). The trait is spelled `Show` and the
; method `show` deliberately: the preference is keyed on the METHOD
; name the builtin owns, and nothing else about the trait matters.
;
; Three renderings, measured by length, summed into the exit status:
;
;   "custom"        6   the instance for `Custom`, not the derived `Warm`
;   "{c = Cold}"   10   a `Custom` INSIDE a struct renders structurally -
;                       the instance is consulted at the call and never
;                       for a nested field, so a nested rendering does
;                       not depend on machinery that is on its way out
;   "(Some 3)"      8   no instance for `Option`: the builtin answers
;                       24
;
; This is a trait fixture and goes with the trait machinery when that
; is deleted; until then it is the one runtime pin of the preference.
; `Fmt` is loaded because the builtin is a rewrite onto it - `3`
; inside `(Some 3)` is `fmtInt` - and nothing else here imports it.
(import Str)

(import Fmt)

(trait (Show a) where
  (show :: (-> a String)))

(data Custom
  (Warm)
  (Cold))

(impl (Show Custom) where
  ((show (lambda (c) "custom"))))

(struct HasCustom
  (c : Custom))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) (+ (strLen (show Warm)) (+ (strLen (show (HasCustom Cold))) (strLen (show (Some 3))))))
